                  %a{:href => "#strict_groupoid_higher_van_kampen_theorem_for_filtered_spaces"} Strict ∞-groupoid Higher van Kampen Theorem for filtered spaces.
              %li
                %a{:href => "#generalised_svkt_for_models_of_types_brownloday"}
                  Generalised SvKT for models of
                  = succeed "-types" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi n
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              %li
                %a{:href => "#van_kampen_spectral_sequence_artinmazur"} Van Kampen spectral sequence (Artin-Mazur)
              %li
                %a{:href => "#for_objects_in_a_cohesive_topos"}
                  For objects in a cohesive
                  = succeed "-topos" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mn ∞
                          %mo ,
                          %mn 1
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
              %li
                %a{:href => "#examples"} Examples
              %li
                %a{:href => "#References"} References
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %em higher homotopy van Kampen theorem
            is a theorem that asserts that the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type"} homotopy type
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            can be ‘computed’, in some sense, by a suitable
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimit"} colimit
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+colimit"} homotopy colimit
            over the homotopy types / invariants of its pieces.
          %p
            These all in some sense generalize the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/van+Kampen+theorem"} van Kampen theorem
          %h2#for_topological_spaces__the_homotopy_small_simplex_theorem For topological spaces  (The homotopy small simplex theorem.)
          %p
            The general form that this takes is similar to the classical homology small simplex theorem which can be found in many books on homology. This states that given an open cover,
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒰
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{U}
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            = succeed "-small" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒰
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{U}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            induces isomorphisms on all homology groups. It is closely related to results on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/excision"} excision
            and on the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mayer-Vietoris+sequence"} Mayer-Vietoris theorem
          %p The experience from the uses of the homology small simplex theorem suggests that it may be most useful when used in conjunction with specific invariants of homotopy types. (Note excision fails for homotopy groups and so one has to be more subtle.) The precise relationship to the other forms of generalised van Kampen theorem has yet to be determined.
          %h3#general_statement_small_simplex_theorem_aka_generalised_svkt General statement (Small simplex theorem aka generalised SvKT)
          .un_theorem
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              be a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Op
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Op(X)
              for its
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+open+subsets"} category of open subsets
              and let
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi χ
                    %mo :
                    %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %mi Op
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\chi : C \to Op(X)
            %p
              be a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
              out of a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+category"} small category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              such that
            %ul
              %li
                for each point
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\in X
                the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+subcategory"} full subcategory
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi C
                        %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C_x
                of objects
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi c
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
                such that
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi χ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi c
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \chi(c)
                contains
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
                has a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+homotopy+equivalence"} weakly
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/contractible"} contractible
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nerve"} nerve
            %p Then:
            %p
              the canonical morphism in
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sSet"} sSet
              out of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimit"} colimit
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %munder
                        %mi lim
                        %mo →
                    %mi Sing
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi χ
                    %mo →
                    %mi Sing
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    {\lim_\to} Sing \circ \chi \to Sing(X)
            %p
              into the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singular+simplicial+complex"} singular simplicial complex
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              exhibits
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Sing
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sing(X)
              as the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+colimit"} homotopy colimit
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi hocolim
                      %mi Sing
                      %mo ∘
                      %mi χ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} hocolim Sing \circ \chi
          %p
            This is theorem A.1.1 in (
            %a{:href => "#Lurie"}> Lurie
            ).
          %p
            A related theorem is given by (
            %a{:href => "#DuggerIsaksen"}> Dugger-Isaksen 2004
            ) and earlier versions were given by
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/G.+Segal"} G. Segal
          %p
            This form of the general type of result gives a relationship between the homotopy type of the space and the homotopy types of parts of that space, telling one how they fit together. To get explicit more ‘algebraic’ calculations out it has so far proved necessary to consider spaces with extra structure, such as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/filtered+topological+space"} filtration
            (Brown-Higgins). The explicit algebraic models do not, in general, give a complete classifying invariant of the homotopy types concerned, but that allows much more precise and calculable invariants to be found. In the first ‘strict’ version, the invariant, namely the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+%E2%88%9E-groupoid"} fundamental ∞-groupoid
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+%E2%88%9E-groupoid"} strict ∞-groupoid
            and could be substituted by the corresponding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/crossed+complex"} crossed complex
            (according to your taste and needs!)
          %h2#filtered_space__strict_groupoid_generalisation_of_the_svkt_brownhiggins Filtered space / strict ∞-groupoid generalisation of the SvKT (Brown-Higgins)
          %p
            Suppose
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_*
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/filtered+space"} filtered space
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is the union of the interiors of sets
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi U
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U^i
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i \in I
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi U
                    %mo *
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U^i_*
            be the filtered space given by the intersections
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi U
                    %mi i
                  %mo ∩
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U^i \cap X_n
            for
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo ≥
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \geq 0
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi i
                  %mo ,
                  %mi j
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∈
                  %msup
                    %mi I
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d=(i,j) \in I^2
            we write
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi U
                    %mi d
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U^d
            for
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi U
                      %mi i
                    %mo ∩
                    %msup
                      %mi U
                      %mi j
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U^i \cap U^j
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⨆
                    %mrow
                      %mi d
                      %mo ∈
                      %msup
                        %mi I
                        %mn 2
                  %msubsup
                    %mi U
                    %mo *
                    %mi d
                  %msubsup
                    %mo ⇉
                    %mi b
                    %mi a
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⨆
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi I
                  %msubsup
                    %mi U
                    %mo *
                    %mi i
                  %msup
                    %mo →
                    %mi c
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mo *
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bigsqcup_{d \in I^2} U^d_* \rightrightarrows ^a_b \bigsqcup _{i \in I} U^i_* \to ^c X_*.
          .un_theorem
            %h6#strict_groupoid_higher_van_kampen_theorem_for_filtered_spaces Strict ∞-groupoid Higher van Kampen Theorem for filtered spaces.
            %p
              If the filtered spaces
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi U
                      %mo *
                      %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U^f_*
              are
              = succeed "s" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connected+filtered+space"} connected filtered space
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi U
                      %mo *
                      %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U^f_*
              of the filtered spaces
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msubsup
                        %mi U
                        %mo *
                        %mi i
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U^i_*
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  (Conn) The filtered space
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi X
                          %mo *
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_*
                  is connected; and
              %li
                %p
                  (Iso) The fundamental
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/crossed+complex"} crossed complex
                  functor
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Π
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi
                  takes the above coequaliser diagram of filtered spaces to a coequaliser diagram of crossed complexes.
          %p
            %strong Remarks
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Note that because
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Π
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi
                uses groupoids, it obviously takes disjoint unions
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⨆
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bigsqcup
                of filtered spaces into disjoint unions (= coproducts)
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⨆
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bigsqcup
                of crossed complexes.
            %li
              %p
                The proof of the theorem is not direct but goes via the fundamental cubical
                = succeed "-groupoid" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ω
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
                %em algebraic inverse to subdivision
                and of
                = succeed "." do
                  %em commutative cube
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/van+Kampen+theorem"} van Kampen theorem
                for the
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+groupoid"} fundamental groupoid
            %li
              %p
                Applications of this theorem include many basic facts in algebraic topology, such as the Relative Hurewicz Theorem, the Brouwer degree theorem, and new nonabelian results on 2nd relative homotopy groups, not of course obtainable by the traditional wholly abelian methods. No use is made of
                %em singular homology theory
                or of
                = succeed "." do
                  %em simplicial approximation
            %li
              %p This result includes the crossed module version of the generalised SvKT. This was the first extension in this direction. It handles relative homotopy groups. That version is also a special case of the next of this type of result given by the Brown-Loday theory.
          %h2#generalised_svkt_for_models_of_types_brownloday
            Generalised SvKT for models of
            = succeed "-types" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %p
            After
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Loday"} Loday
            had proved that
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cat-n-group"} cat-n-group
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+n-type"} homotopy (n+1)-type
          %h2#van_kampen_spectral_sequence_artinmazur Van Kampen spectral sequence (Artin-Mazur)
          %p
            Artin and Mazur proved the existence of a spectral sequence determined by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cech+complex"} Cech complex
            of an open cover. This converges to the homotopy groups of the big space. There does not seem to have been any study done of the relationship of their results with the others mentioned above. Their methods are very similar to those used for the Dugger-Isaksen small simplex theorem, however.
          %h2#for_objects_in_a_cohesive_topos
            For objects in a cohesive
            = succeed "-topos" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
          %p
            In a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesive+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-topos"} cohesive (∞,1)-topos
            higher van Kampen theorems hold in great generality.
          %p
            See the section
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "http://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/cohesive+(infinity%2C1)-topos#vanKampenTheorem"} cohesive (∞,1)-topos – van Kampen theorem
          %p
            In particular for the cohesive
            = succeed "-topos" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9ETopGrpd"} ∞TopGrpd
            of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+%E2%88%9E-groupoid"} topological ∞-groupoid
            %a{:href => "#ForTopSpaces"} topological higher van Kampen theorem
            discussed above.
          %h2#examples Examples
          %p
            Here is one application in dimension 2 not easily obtainable by traditional
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+topology"} algebraic topology
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                  %mo →
                  %mi P
                  %mo →
                  %mi Q
                  %mo →
                  %mi R
                  %mo →
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \to P \to Q \to R \to 0
            be an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exact+sequence"} exact sequence
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+groups"} abelian groups
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            be the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mapping+cone"} mapping cone
            of the induced map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi P
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Q
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K(P,1) \to K(Q,1)
            of
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eilenberg-Mac+Lane+space"} Eilenberg-Mac Lane space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/crossed+module"} crossed module
            representing the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+2-type"} homotopy 2-type
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                  %mo :
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mi Q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu: C \to Q
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is abelian and is the direct sum
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ⊕
                    %mrow
                      %mi r
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi R
                  %msup
                    %mi P
                    %mi r
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \oplus_{r \in R} P^r
            of copies of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            one for each
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi r
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r \in R
            and the action of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q
            is via
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            and permutes the copies by
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi p
                    %mo ,
                    %mi r
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi s
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi p
                    %mo ,
                    %mi r
                    %mo +
                    %mi s
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (p,r)^s=(p,r+s)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Q
                  %mo ,
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P,Q,R
            nonabelian are do-able, more complicated, and certainly
            %strong not
            obtainable by traditional methods.
          %h2#References References
          %p Various versions exist, generalising the classical theorem.
          %p
            The homology small simplex theorem is to be found, for instance, in
            = succeed "\u2018s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Spanier"} Spanier
          %p
            The homotopy version for topological spaces and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+infinity-groupoid"} fundamental infinity-groupoid
            functor is discussed in
            %a{:href => "http://www.math.harvard.edu/~lurie/papers/DAG-VI.pdf#page=134"} Appendix A.1
            of
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacob+Lurie"}> Jacob Lurie
              ,
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ek-Algebras"} Ek-Algebras
              (now part of
              = succeed ")" do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higher+Algebra"} Higher Algebra
          %p A very similar result occurs before as prop. A.5 in
          %ul#Segal
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Graeme+Segal"}> Graeme Segal
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Classifying spaces related to foliations
          %p
            An important central lemma (the
            = succeed ")" do
              %em small simplices theorem
          %ul#DuggerIsaksen
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Daniel+Dugger"}> Daniel Dugger
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Daniel+Isaksen"} Daniel Isaksen
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  Topological hypercovers and
                  = succeed "-realizations" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi 𝔸
                            %mn 1
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{A}^1
          %p where many similar results are discussed.
          %p
            The version for filtered topological spaces and the strict homotopy
            = succeed "-groupoid" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ronnie+Brown"} Ronnie Brown
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Philip+Higgins"} Philip Higgins
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ronnie+Brown"}> Ronnie Brown
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Philip+Higgins"} Philip Higgins
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Rafael+Sivera"} Rafael Sivera
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nonabelian+Algebraic+Topology"} Nonabelian Algebraic Topology
          %p The Brown-Loday results can be found in
          %ul
            %li
              R. Brown and
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/J.-L.+Loday"} J.-L. Loday
              = succeed "," do
                %em Homotopical excision, and Hurewicz theorems for n-cubes of spaces
          %p and
          %ul
            %li
              R. Brown and
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/J.-L.+Loday"} J.-L. Loday
              = succeed "," do
                %em Van Kampen Theorems for diagrams of spaces
          %p The paper of Artin and Mazur is
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M.+Artin"} M. Artin
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/B.+Mazur"} B. Mazur
              = succeed "," do
                %em On the van Kampen theorem
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "http://www.maths.ed.ac.uk/~aar/topology/Topology%20Vol%2005/Artin_On-the-van-Kampen-theorem_1966.pdf"} Topology, vol. 5 (1966), pp. 179 - 189
